1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,065
Welcome to practicals thirteen and fourteen:

2
00:00:03,065 --> 00:00:05,085
data visualization in Python.

3
00:00:05,085 --> 00:00:14,697
We cover the Grammar of Graphics, good versus misleading plots, visualizing non-numeric data, dashboard logic, and storytelling with data.

4
00:00:14,697 --> 00:00:21,384
We use the Superstore retail orders dataset, with nine thousand nine hundred ninety four orders.

5
00:00:22,184 --> 00:00:23,130
First, setup.

6
00:00:23,130 --> 00:00:31,931
Import pandas, numpy, seaborn and matplotlib, plus squarify for treemaps, network x for network diagrams, and word cloud.

7
00:00:31,931 --> 00:00:37,460
Load the Superstore file the same way as in earlier labs, with one addition:

8
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:45,752
convert the order date from plain text into a real datetime type, which we need for the monthly trend chart later.

9
00:00:46,552 --> 00:00:47,450
Section two:

10
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,717
the Grammar of Graphics, building a chart layer by layer.

11
00:00:51,717 --> 00:00:54,486
Every chart is composed the same way:

12
00:00:54,486 --> 00:01:02,495
data, then aesthetics such as x, y and colour, then geometry, the plot type, then scale labels and a theme.

13
00:01:02,495 --> 00:01:07,360
Once you can name these layers, you can build or debug any chart.

14
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,167
Here is one chart mapped to the grammar.

15
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:13,647
The data layer is the data frame.

16
00:01:13,647 --> 00:01:19,661
The aesthetics layer is x equals category, y equals sales, and colour by region.

17
00:01:19,661 --> 00:01:25,224
The geometry layer is the bar plot itself, with the mean as the estimator.

18
00:01:25,224 --> 00:01:27,554
Then we add a title and labels.

19
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:33,192
Reading the code layer by layer is exactly how you would build or debug it.

20
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:36,878
Facets repeat the same chart once per group.

21
00:01:36,878 --> 00:01:49,670
With col equals region and col wrap equals two, we get one small bar chart of average profit by segment for each region, arranged in a grid, from one line of code instead of four separate charts.

22
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:54,656
This is the grammar's answer to how do I compare a chart across many groups.

23
00:01:55,456 --> 00:01:56,512
Section three:

24
00:01:56,512 --> 00:01:58,699
good versus misleading plots.

25
00:01:58,699 --> 00:02:05,639
The same data, drawn two different ways, can leave a viewer with very different impressions.

26
00:02:05,639 --> 00:02:09,184
The most common culprit is the y axis baseline.

27
00:02:09,984 --> 00:02:19,138
On the left, the y axis is forced to start at point one oh, making Central's discount look several times taller than West's.

28
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:25,191
On the right, the axis starts at zero and shows the true, much smaller difference.

29
00:02:25,191 --> 00:02:27,184
Same data, different story.

30
00:02:27,184 --> 00:02:31,392
When bars encode a quantity, the axis must start at zero.

31
00:02:32,192 --> 00:02:33,144
Section four:

32
00:02:33,144 --> 00:02:35,266
visualizing non-numeric data.

33
00:02:35,266 --> 00:02:44,416
Numeric summaries like mean and correlation do not apply to categories, hierarchies or text, so each needs its own technique.

34
00:02:44,416 --> 00:02:47,271
For categories, use a sorted bar chart.

35
00:02:47,271 --> 00:02:56,275
The order argument sorts bars by value instead of alphabetically, so Phones and Chairs lead and Fasteners and Labels trail.

36
00:02:56,275 --> 00:03:00,008
Sorting lets the pattern make the point on its own.

37
00:03:00,808 --> 00:03:03,527
For hierarchical data, use a treemap.

38
00:03:03,527 --> 00:03:09,479
Squarify draws one rectangle per category, with area proportional to total sales.

39
00:03:09,479 --> 00:03:19,840
Technology, furniture and office supplies are close in size, which a treemap makes easy to compare without reading exact numbers off an axis.

40
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,555
For text, use a word cloud.

41
00:03:22,555 --> 00:03:31,564
Join every product name into one long string, and word cloud counts word frequency and sizes each word by how often it appears.

42
00:03:31,564 --> 00:03:38,232
It is good for a quick skim of what dominates a text field, though not for precise comparison.

43
00:03:39,032 --> 00:03:41,890
For relationships, use a network diagram.

44
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:48,095
We connect each of the top fifteen customers to every sub-category they have bought from.

45
00:03:48,095 --> 00:03:54,090
That makes a bipartite network, with two kinds of nodes, customers and sub-categories.

46
00:03:54,090 --> 00:03:59,528
It shows which sub-categories are shared across customers and which are niche.

47
00:04:00,328 --> 00:04:01,243
Section five:

48
00:04:01,243 --> 00:04:02,370
dashboard logic.

49
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:07,790
A dashboard is several panels sharing one figure, organised around a purpose:

50
00:04:07,790 --> 00:04:10,818
key numbers first, supporting detail below.

51
00:04:10,818 --> 00:04:12,155
Here is the layout.

52
00:04:12,155 --> 00:04:23,842
Add gridspec reserves a three by three grid, with a shorter top row for the three K P I cards, and a taller row below with a wide trend panel and a smaller mix panel.

53
00:04:23,842 --> 00:04:27,080
Each add subplot claims a region of that grid.

54
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:29,966
Then each panel is filled the normal way:

55
00:04:29,966 --> 00:04:41,934
ax text for the K P I cards showing orders, total sales and total profit, a seaborn line plot for the monthly sales trend, and a bar plot for the sales share by category.

56
00:04:41,934 --> 00:04:48,904
The only new idea is that every plotting call targets a specific panel instead of the whole figure.

57
00:04:49,704 --> 00:04:50,555
Section six:

58
00:04:50,555 --> 00:04:52,187
storytelling with data.

59
00:04:52,187 --> 00:04:55,168
First, highlight the one bar that matters.

60
00:04:55,168 --> 00:05:02,263
A short list comprehension picks one strong colour for Central and a muted grey for every other bar:

61
00:05:02,263 --> 00:05:03,541
gray out the rest.

62
00:05:03,541 --> 00:05:12,552
And the chart title states the insight, Central's discount rate stands out from the rest, instead of just naming the variables.

63
00:05:13,352 --> 00:05:17,120
Second, annotate the key moment directly on the chart.

64
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:25,215
Idx min finds the exact month where profit was lowest, so we let the data locate the annotation instead of guessing.

65
00:05:25,215 --> 00:05:29,681
Then annotate draws a label and an arrow pointing at that point.

66
00:05:29,681 --> 00:05:34,496
A short label at the key moment saves the viewer from hunting for it.

67
00:05:35,296 --> 00:05:36,384
Section seven:

68
00:05:36,384 --> 00:05:40,192
the same techniques on a second dataset, Titanic.

69
00:05:40,192 --> 00:05:42,913
The same grammar pattern as before:

70
00:05:42,913 --> 00:05:50,296
data, aesthetics with passenger class on x, fare on y and sex as colour, and bars for geometry.

71
00:05:51,096 --> 00:05:56,903
And the same treemap pattern, now showing the relative size of each passenger class.

72
00:05:56,903 --> 00:06:02,640
Swap the data frame and column names, and the same techniques apply to any dataset.

73
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:05,130
To practise, try these.

74
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:11,742
Rebuild the grammar bar chart with region on the x axis and see whether the story changes.

75
00:06:11,742 --> 00:06:16,297
Make a treemap of sub-category sales within a single category.

76
00:06:16,297 --> 00:06:23,718
And redraw the discount chart with an honest axis and a highlighted bar, then write an insight title.

77
00:06:23,718 --> 00:06:35,988
The code patterns in this lab, layered charts, honest axes, treemaps, word clouds, network diagrams, gridspec dashboards and annotated highlights, are general purpose.

78
00:06:35,988 --> 00:06:40,323
Swap the data and the same techniques apply to any dataset.

79
00:06:40,323 --> 00:06:41,792
Thanks for watching.

